course: biology
lesson: 155
title: The Amazon river: pink dolphins, flooded forest, giant fish and piranhas
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Amazon is the biggest river on Earth by the water it carries, and every rainy season it rises so high that fish swim among the tree tops of a drowned forest.
explain: In that brown water a pink dolphin, the boto, twists between branches after fish, and a giant arapaima comes up to gulp air with a loud cough.
explain: Cartoons show piranhas stripping a cow to bones in seconds; real piranhas are mostly timid shoaling fish and scavengers, and local children swim in piranha rivers every day.
explain: Near the city of Manaus a dark river like tea and a pale river like milky coffee run side by side for kilometres without mixing, because they differ in temperature, speed and what is dissolved in them.
fact: The boto, or Amazon river dolphin, can turn its head because its neck bones are not joined, and older botos are often the pinkest because scraped skin heals with pink scar tissue.
fact: Every rainy season the Amazon rises by up to ten metres or more and floods huge areas of forest for months.
fact: In the flooded forest fish like the tambaqui eat fallen fruit and nuts and spread the seeds, doing the job birds do on land.
fact: The arapaima, one of the biggest freshwater fish in the world, must come up every ten to twenty minutes to breathe air, because warm flood water holds little oxygen, and its scales are so tough piranhas struggle to bite them.
fact: Giant otters live in noisy family groups of about six to ten that gang up to drive off a caiman, a kind of crocodile, and each has a throat patch as unique as a fingerprint.
fact: Piranhas have razor-sharp teeth and tear at dead or injured fish, but most are timid scavengers that shoal for safety.
fact: The pororoca is a tidal wave that pushes up the mouth of the Amazon when a strong high tide meets the river, and surfers have ridden it for over thirty minutes.
fact: The giant water lily Victoria has floating leaves up to about three metres across, held up by air-filled ribs and guarded by spines underneath; a big leaf can hold a small child if the weight is spread evenly.
fact: The matamata turtle looks like dead leaves and catches fish by suddenly opening its huge mouth so water and fish rush in, and swallows each meal whole.
fact: The Amazon basin has well over two thousand known kinds of fish, more than the whole Atlantic Ocean, and new ones are found every year.
fact: The Amazon carries about a fifth of all the river water that flows into the world's seas.
fact: At the Meeting of the Waters, the dark Rio Negro and the pale Solimões run beside each other for several kilometres before mixing; the Rio Negro is dark like tea from stuff dissolved out of rotting leaves.
fact: Kingfishers plunge in beak first, and Japan's bullet train was given a kingfisher-shaped nose to make it quieter.
fact: Young hoatzins have claws on their wings, so after dropping into the water to escape a snake they swim and climb back up to the nest.
exercise: Why can the boto twist its head between branches? | Its neck bones are not joined together | It has no bones at all | Its head is on a spring | It turns its whole body round instead of its head
exercise: Why do older botos often look pinker? | Scraped skin heals with pink scar tissue | They eat pink shrimps all day, and the colour goes into their skin | They blush when they see boats | They are sunburnt
exercise: How much can the Amazon rise in the rainy season? | Up to ten metres or more | Only about ten centimetres | About one metre, the same as a tall dog | About one kilometre or more
exercise: How do tambaqui fish help the flooded forest? | They eat fallen fruit and nuts and spread the seeds | They eat all the dead leaves | They dig holes for new trees | They keep the water warm with their bodies at night
exercise: Why does the arapaima come up and gulp at the surface? | It breathes air every ten to twenty minutes | It is catching flies | It is calling to its friends further down the river | It is cooling down
exercise: How do giant otters deal with a caiman? | The family gangs up, screaming and biting, to drive it off | They hide high up in the tree tops and keep very quiet until it goes away | They pretend to be logs | They each run away alone
exercise: What are most piranhas really like? | Timid shoaling fish that often scavenge | Fish that can eat a whole cow in a few seconds | Lone hunters that chase swimmers | Plant eaters with no teeth
exercise: What is the pororoca? | A tidal wave that pushes up the river from the sea | A giant river dolphin | A kind of flooded forest that grows along the banks in the rainy season | A storm of piranhas
exercise: How big can the leaf of the giant water lily Victoria grow? | Up to about three metres across | About as big as a grown-up's hand | About as big as a dinner plate, no bigger | About thirty metres across, like a field
exercise: What keeps the giant lily leaf afloat? | Ribs full of air pockets underneath | A layer of oil on top | Fish holding it up | A long stiff stalk that holds it up from the bottom of the river
exercise: How does a matamata turtle catch a fish? | It suddenly opens its huge mouth and sucks the fish in | It chases it at high speed | It stings it with the long sharp spine on the end of its tail | It catches it with its claws
exercise: About how many known kinds of fish live in the Amazon basin? | Well over two thousand | About twenty kinds in all | Only about two hundred kinds | About two million kinds
exercise: How much of the river water flowing into the world's seas comes from the Amazon? | About a fifth | About half of it | About a hundredth | Almost all of it
exercise: Why do the Rio Negro and the Solimões run side by side without mixing at first? | They differ in temperature, speed and dissolved stuff | A wall of rock under the water keeps the two rivers apart | One river flows backwards | Fish keep them apart
exercise: Why is the Rio Negro dark like tea? | Stuff from rotting leaves is dissolved in it | It is full of fine black sand washed down from the mountains | It is very deep, so no sunlight reaches the bottom | It carries black oil seeping up from under the ground
exercise: How does a young hoatzin get back to its nest after dropping into the river? | It swims and climbs up using claws on its wings | It flies straight up | Its mother carries it in her beak | It waits in the nest until the flood water lifts it up
test: In a cartoon a cow steps into a river and a piranha swarm leaves only bones in seconds. What is true? | The scene is a myth; most piranhas are timid scavengers and people swim among them | Exactly what happens every day | Piranhas eat only cows | Piranhas live only in cartoons and films, never in real rivers
test: «pink dolphin?? no way, dolphins are grey, that's fake» What is right? | The boto really exists and turns pinker with age | Right, it is a painted dolphin | Right, pink dolphins live only in zoos where they are fed pink fish | It is a pink fish, not a dolphin
test: A family of 6 giant otters, sorry, 7, stands on the bank. How many legs altogether? | 28 | 24 | 14 | 35
test: A boto, an arapaima and a giant otter swim past. How many tails? | 3 | 2 | 1 | 6
test: What would happen if the floods stopped coming to the Amazon forest? | Fish could no longer feed on fruit and spread seeds, and many trees and fish would suffer | More fish would move up to live in the tree tops instead | Nothing at all would change | The river would get much deeper
test: «a fish that breathes air? fish breathe water, mate» What is right? | The arapaima must gulp air because warm flood water holds little oxygen | Right, it is just showing off | It is a dolphin, not a fish | It only breathes air on land, when it crawls out of the river to rest
test: «my brother wants to sit on a lily leaf» What can the giant lily Victoria leaf hold? | A small child's weight, if spread evenly | Nothing heavier than a frog, or the leaf sinks | A car | Only water drops
test: A tour guide says «surfers ride the river here». How can that be? | The pororoca tidal wave rolls up the river from the sea | The river flows uphill at night | Boats make the waves | It is a joke, because rivers never have any waves
test: A heap of brown leaves in the mud suddenly swallows a fish. What was it? | A matamata turtle | A piranha | A giant otter | A boto
test: «that black river's dirty, don't swim, it's oil» What is the dark colour really? | Dissolved stuff from rotting leaves, like tea | Oil from deep wells | Black paint from a factory | Ink squirted by octopuses that swim up the river
test: The bullet train's nose was copied from which Amazon diver? | A kingfisher's beak | A river dolphin's snout | A piranha's sharp teeth | An arapaima's scales
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the piranhas of the Amazon. | The majority are timid shoaling fish, frequently scavenging, and are seldom dangerous to swimmers | Shoals habitually reduce whole cattle to skeletons within seconds whenever they enter the water | They subsist exclusively upon the flesh of mammals | They are entirely without teeth
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the arapaima rises periodically to the surface. | It is obliged to breathe air, the warm flood water holding little oxygen | It thereby feeds upon insects alighting upon the surface of the water during the night | It thereby signals to other members of its species | It thereby lowers its body temperature
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the pororoca? | A tidal wave advancing up the mouth of the river against its current | A species of freshwater dolphin of a pink colour | A tract of seasonally flooded forest | A shoal of predatory fish
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the Rio Negro and the Solimões flow alongside one another for several kilometres prior to mixing. | The two waters differ in temperature, velocity and dissolved substances | A submerged wall separates the two channels | The one river flows in the contrary direction to the other, so that they cannot join | The fish of each river prevent intermingling
